#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087628 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "11f5bedf-cfc8-5ace-b080-5ec30b406a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7436" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/0a/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ 8 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "29cfe1ff-ca64-51f9-8da1-5d0f798711f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7437" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "07678ad9-a16f-5fd2-8e39-85376aa9112e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7442" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75998071-befb-5489-be0c-bbf3e1494aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239070} {:answer/id #uuid "88eea04a-d8a1-51c3-97d8-6e529bd9b97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239247} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ecb594-d2f1-55cd-8617-ef560cc14075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239488} {:answer/id #uuid "c123727a-d48d-5583-8a9c-797904a46c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239743} {:answer/id #uuid "c7df434f-7524-5eb1-9b00-8cdfa267e631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c123727a-d48d-5583-8a9c-797904a46c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239743} :answer-field/id #uuid "336c67f8-b80c-57f5-b2fd-845c6ff24aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f65595d4-58fb-5ba2-ab22-c9fc4bc25274" :question/math-academy-id "q-106412" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ -8 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7ddbe0-edeb-5af4-acdc-0e8bdedd7785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535455} {:answer/id #uuid "2637c6d9-f699-5e24-af7a-329870be2fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535495} {:answer/id #uuid "369283f1-0003-5dfd-b8bf-780c8fe3b4e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{4}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535518} {:answer/id #uuid "512a7ed1-b0bf-5dd9-9978-7ed2decafc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535559} {:answer/id #uuid "82e84ea1-476d-5fa4-8ca5-a30056317436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2637c6d9-f699-5e24-af7a-329870be2fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535495} :answer-field/id #uuid "709bb002-42ed-5076-9f07-4d7555384dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "476be737-90ba-5bfe-8a20-c650319e8b3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106414" :question/problem "![](images/89/8934e5b6135b97b845dad9a6b87056d2c228fd2424d3a72141df694d46ccf7c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-6,12).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-6,12),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=12$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6=2pt \\\\ 12=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040cac0d-ceb3-5aa4-b3ee-09a2f428db68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535442} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1afc7d-7411-5523-bc97-b014798b52ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535656} {:answer/id #uuid "a3464f1b-c16b-5140-b363-184627cbe999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535688} {:answer/id #uuid "b070a6db-e004-52b8-9c3d-4d905814e58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535705} {:answer/id #uuid "e0245b50-a16f-522a-94f9-37b20af9e5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{9}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3464f1b-c16b-5140-b363-184627cbe999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535688} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0ba07f-9ec1-55d7-ab0e-5b909c4fa1a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9466ac77-f4bf-5bd1-8cef-81367e73b3b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106443" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{1}{6}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-4x,\\,t=-6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =36{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{y}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e2d09a-689d-5e58-a00e-c11460b13e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e2d09a-689d-5e58-a00e-c11460b13e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377045} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad9a621-dd17-56d4-9033-7136556fc8ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377044} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "843a74a1-39ae-5459-aac3-eba8e54c75d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "843a74a1-39ae-5459-aac3-eba8e54c75d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377047} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0622f5b-ad86-55ca-876c-6179afbb1ce5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659b155d-7a86-5ce4-a60f-a888671f046c" :question/math-academy-id "q-280198" :question/problem "![](images/23/23fa9b0f268e953cf9a2b770c3a3cb83c64fa0217a55e3a5313c81bf6933fc0a.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,2),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2pt \\\\ 2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2pt \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\\,y=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34dc9e34-88b2-585e-b8f0-9828d32d21e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34dc9e34-88b2-585e-b8f0-9828d32d21e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535515} :answer-field/id #uuid "852f5335-7100-5af8-83e3-1bcac38ee757" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "792a7020-748e-5273-a5c8-4d48fd7db315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "792a7020-748e-5273-a5c8-4d48fd7db315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535622} :answer-field/id #uuid "f288405a-29b1-5b71-8025-b48b0179b10a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5daa6476-7e34-5e5c-afc2-27c877b6a525" :question/math-academy-id "q-280220" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-8,8).$ Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-8,8),$ we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(2)t,\\,y=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ca048e-9b50-5dcf-9df4-14fad3d92fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ca048e-9b50-5dcf-9df4-14fad3d92fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293105} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e581868-46f8-543d-80e1-59d5f2c1df19" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "391fb9e5-7f0b-525a-9579-6bcb09eaadd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "391fb9e5-7f0b-525a-9579-6bcb09eaadd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293171} :answer-field/id #uuid "556a7d43-607a-51f8-bbc7-32726ebb62ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09e9d4e7-4fc4-5d2a-8e90-c6b221db0edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-280223" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-8x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-8x \\\\ 4p & =-8 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-2{t}^{2},\\,y=-4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6a52e9f-190c-5ab9-a11a-f768fda6293e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6a52e9f-190c-5ab9-a11a-f768fda6293e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535788} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa7d66b-3573-5d07-a0e0-9c784e111c46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa7375ba-60b0-59ae-8b9b-5dbd4e3f033d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa7375ba-60b0-59ae-8b9b-5dbd4e3f033d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535822} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17e1296-2fea-5348-b461-c3962ef4a04f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8855f85e-af22-5017-b471-837c5222e69b" :question/math-academy-id "q-280225" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-24x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-24x \\\\ 4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-6){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-6)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-6{t}^{2},\\,y=-12t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfc786c5-1bc9-5a98-bf8d-bbc825fe9cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293805} {:answer/id #uuid "88b60145-74d7-5125-9d2f-627fd47ed384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88b60145-74d7-5125-9d2f-627fd47ed384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535652} :answer-field/id #uuid "07742959-c980-5ce9-8c75-4087f38f7a10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0cb4025-50ee-5702-890c-3a56cbc8ed9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0cb4025-50ee-5702-890c-3a56cbc8ed9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293812} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ab8ad5-15cd-521e-8260-3cbd624c5fe6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "661e8b2f-b39b-52c7-a534-20b19b10d577" :question/math-academy-id "q-280226" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=32x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =32x \\\\ 4p & =32 \\\\ p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(8){t}^{2},\\,y=2(8)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=8{t}^{2},\\,y=16t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b142e8-d51f-5a1b-ace6-11e17bdf26a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b142e8-d51f-5a1b-ace6-11e17bdf26a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292972} :answer-field/id #uuid "9af6deca-2a10-5fc3-bd8b-495794517f17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294486} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1b74dbe-0c40-55e8-b6f9-1afe703e8f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1b74dbe-0c40-55e8-b6f9-1afe703e8f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293816} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7dbeb64-58f8-574e-b5ca-1af2fbe26d0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "101558b0-e03b-5b3f-a1c3-2d14536fe534" :question/math-academy-id "q-280233" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -4 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{4}{5}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{4}{5})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{8}{5}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897301ec-b17d-5ec4-950b-678e2f9e2a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897301ec-b17d-5ec4-950b-678e2f9e2a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535654} :answer-field/id #uuid "23812876-0f00-5f63-a067-548a46195934" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "117d2bbe-700d-5500-84d8-cda2c172b75c" :question/math-academy-id "q-280382" :question/problem "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=15{t}^{2},\\,y=3t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{15},\\,t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{15}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{x}{15} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec9f779c-6b56-5a47-b5ce-8ecc7e15bfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=24{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377902} {:answer/id #uuid "faf2ab90-1944-5dbc-a1cb-77420b1da390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377903} {:answer/id #uuid "24c1fb89-78ee-5403-94f4-14694d89539b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377904} {:answer/id #uuid "ceda9423-24e0-57eb-b69d-8e72177caace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377905} {:answer/id #uuid "143d7c5c-3a49-594c-b23d-a77f8e9fe684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf2ab90-1944-5dbc-a1cb-77420b1da390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377903} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ebdc11-f05d-553b-a7a6-a6b674bb37b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bf28229-05a6-5b2e-8652-9947e15e4e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-84672" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d67b2209b622bd26ce7e4ee488ca02208bb120fcd0ab01c602db67da1291dc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(6,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,3),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6 &=2pt \\\\ 3 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ 3 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(3)t,\\,y=(3){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=6t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2771000a-6db8-5877-b7c6-5b7844a78fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535497} {:answer/id #uuid "616c0775-e808-578f-831f-06059bc472d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535587} {:answer/id #uuid "b2849469-2ae6-5d77-81c9-093078f83f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535708} {:answer/id #uuid "bed7ea9b-4c8f-5ee5-8da5-9881fef75d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{4}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535727} {:answer/id #uuid "cf409848-806c-52aa-b07d-5b0b88efdbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2771000a-6db8-5877-b7c6-5b7844a78fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535497} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f25c337-6e13-59ee-b2b3-9226f27c88ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf998376-0b5d-5f0e-ac72-6bbc0864becd" :question/math-academy-id "q-84714" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-10x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-10x \\\\ 4p & =-10 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{5}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{5}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-5t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a0afec-500e-53d1-9a4b-5db662c57646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352560} {:answer/id #uuid "9edaea82-23aa-5e90-8155-68a6076e9265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352926} {:answer/id #uuid "a750d0fc-c06e-542b-98a2-8b5abc19d91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352985} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e7a34a-2bb9-5dc8-978a-a104fe2d99d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353400} {:answer/id #uuid "f160fdd7-fa88-59e4-883d-5682e447cc0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71a0afec-500e-53d1-9a4b-5db662c57646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352560} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb88179f-11d7-5050-8c12-182fcc837ccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50547e51-0957-5746-a2ba-be9442cc3fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-84731" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4x,\\,t=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239ab912-cfd2-52df-b992-7ed2e7c2727b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351890} {:answer/id #uuid "48369b80-33d6-5dff-b595-9ed79809a5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352233} {:answer/id #uuid "48a82cc1-e586-531a-929d-1d8bc4d7682d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352235} {:answer/id #uuid "af0f17ad-de53-583e-867b-c6b65dcb8a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353053} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe591b0-2fa1-5c27-a356-a0fac57fe3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239ab912-cfd2-52df-b992-7ed2e7c2727b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351890} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32942c1-25d1-5332-a4bb-1799eabe3692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae256195-d3f3-53da-b912-0eaa42469a59" :question/math-academy-id "q-84732" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{3},\\,t=\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =12x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=12x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd9ef0b-d572-528c-a599-00ba2e543438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535480} {:answer/id #uuid "41828207-3d09-56c9-a9ee-6957fc1cab42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535536} {:answer/id #uuid "4b96b87b-afac-5cf9-a064-ad7eb9c9ef16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535544} {:answer/id #uuid "74def176-8b40-5a47-a8d8-9b0849a0f8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535615} {:answer/id #uuid "92966b0c-4feb-5a90-a55c-2c0fd4e3536c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41828207-3d09-56c9-a9ee-6957fc1cab42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535536} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b5c3524-dbe9-508f-a227-002a3105ecbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d316b19-46f8-547d-95ad-bbdabb50ffb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84735" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-2x,\\,t=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-2x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-2x.$"}]}
